=== AUDIT_EVIDENCE ===
Two audits drove the implementation: (1) routing.yaml's actual `dest:
positions` entries vs prompt Decision #3's speculative list, and (2)
the codec's Location-handling semantics vs prompt Decision #2's
hypothetical compound `Location` atomic.

(1) routing.yaml — actual dest: positions entries
Command: Select-String -Path internal\tesla\router\routing.yaml -Pattern 'dest:\s*positions' -Context 1,2

Result:
  L436-438:  - field: GpsHeading        / dest: positions / column: heading_deg
  L439-441:  - field: GpsState          / dest: positions / column: gps_state
  L538-540:  - field: LocationLatitude  / dest: positions / column: lat
  L541-543:  - field: LocationLongitude / dest: positions / column: lng

  Total: 4 routed fields. NEITHER VehicleSpeed NOR a compound
  `Location` field appears with dest: positions. Prompt Decision #3's
  list ("Location, VehicleSpeed, Gps, Altitude, Heading") is stale.
  VehicleSpeed routes to drive_telemetry per a separate routing.yaml
  block; Altitude is not currently routed; Heading is split into
  GpsHeading (which IS routed) and other compass-shaped atomics that
  are not.

(2) codec — actual Location-flattening semantics
Command: grep flattenLocation internal\tesla\codec\codec.go internal\tesla\codec\flatten.go

Result:
  internal/tesla/codec/codec.go:160 dispatches Location to
  flattenLocation(field, value).
  internal/tesla/codec/flatten.go:18-23: flattenLocation always emits
  TWO atomics — one with field "LocationLatitude" + float64 value, one
  with field "LocationLongitude" + float64 value. The codec NEVER
  emits a single Atomic with field == "Location" and a compound
  LocationValue. The hypothetical `codec.LocationValue` referenced by
  prompt Decision #2 does not exist in internal/tesla/codec.

(3) routing.yaml architectural intent
The block at routing.yaml L530-537 (the comment immediately preceding
the LocationLatitude / LocationLongitude entries) explicitly designates
this writer as the pair-up point:

  "the codec emits both in the same payload with a shared EmittedAt
   timestamp, and the positions writer (added in prompt 0061) buffers
   one half until the other arrives so the (lat, lng) pair lands on a
   single row. Routing is field-static per ADR-004 #8 — the pair-up
   logic lives in the writer, not here."

This is the AUTHORITATIVE specification and it directly contradicts
prompt Decision #2 (which assumed routing would carry compound
Location and the writer would only need to bind two columns from one
Atomic). The routing.yaml comment supersedes the prompt because the
codec's flatten behaviour is locked by phase-42 prompt 0030 and
cannot be undone — Location compounds are decomposed BEFORE the
router sees them.

(4) migration 000182 — actual schema
File: migrations/000182_positions_si.up.sql
Columns:
  vehicle_id    BIGINT             NOT NULL  (PK)
  ts            TIMESTAMPTZ        NOT NULL  (PK)
  lat           DOUBLE PRECISION   NOT NULL  <-- the constraint that
  lng           DOUBLE PRECISION   NOT NULL  <-- breaks per-column upsert
  altitude_m    DOUBLE PRECISION
  speed_mps     DOUBLE PRECISION
  heading_deg   DOUBLE PRECISION
  gps_state     TEXT
  odometer_m    DOUBLE PRECISION
  est_range_m   DOUBLE PRECISION
  rated_range_m DOUBLE PRECISION
  ideal_range_m DOUBLE PRECISION

  altitude_m / speed_mps / odometer_m / est_range_m / rated_range_m /
  ideal_range_m are present in the schema but are NOT routed to
  positions in routing.yaml — they are populated either by other
  writers (drive_telemetry for speed_mps), via signal_log replays, or
  not at all in this phase. The positions writer must NOT bind those
  columns.

  Decision #4 of the prompt (per-column upsert pattern copied verbatim
  from snapshot_base.go) is INCOMPATIBLE with this schema: an INSERT
  that supplies only `lat` cannot satisfy `lng NOT NULL`, and vice
  versa. The writer must defer flush until BOTH lat and lng are
  observed for the same (vin, ts) — exactly what routing.yaml L530-
  537 mandates.

(5) Predecessor pgxPool interface reuse
The pgxPool interface declared in internal/tesla/router/writers/
snapshot_base.go:22-24 (Exec(ctx, sql, args...) -> pgconn.CommandTag,
error) is a strict subset of *pgxpool.Pool's surface. The positions
writer reuses it without redeclaration so the existing recorder fake
in snapshot_base_test.go is directly applicable to positions tests.

=== IMPLEMENTATION ===

Files written:

(a) internal/tesla/router/writers/positions_writer.go (~16 KB)

  Public surface:
    func NewPositionsWriter(pool *pgxpool.Pool) router.Writer
      - Locked signature per Decision #1.
      - Panics on nil pool — wiring bug surfaced at process start.

  Package-private constructor:
    func newPositionsWriter(db pgxPool) *positionsWriter
      - Tests inject a recording pgxPool fake without depending on
        a real *pgxpool.Pool.
      - Also the seam for production wiring to override pendingTTL,
        maxPending, evictionInterval, and now without bumping the
        public surface.

  State:
    type positionsWriter struct {
        db                pgxPool
        now               func() time.Time      // injected for tests
        pendingTTL        time.Duration         // default 5 min
        maxPending        int                   // default 100_000
        evictionInterval  time.Duration         // default 30 sec
        mu                sync.Mutex
        pending           map[positionsKey]*positionsPending
        lastEviction      time.Time
    }

    type positionsKey struct {
        vin string
        ts  time.Time   // normalised to UTC + Round(0)
    }

    type positionsPending struct {
        hasLat, hasLng    bool
        lat, lng          float64
        headingDeg        *float64        // nil = not yet observed
        gpsState          *string         // nil = not yet observed
        firstSeenAt       time.Time       // for TTL eviction
    }

  Algorithm of Write (codec.Atomic, router.Entry):

    1. Switch on atom.Field with explicit cases for the 4 routed
       fields. Type-check atom.Value against the column's storage
       type WITHOUT mutating writer state. Reject with a typed error
       on mismatch.

       LocationLatitude  -> requires float64
       LocationLongitude -> requires float64
       GpsHeading        -> requires float32 OR float64 (codec emits
                            float32 via ftproto.Value_FloatValue;
                            float64 accepted defensively for any
                            future protomodel promotion)
       GpsState          -> requires string

       Default case returns the locked error wording from Decision #5:
         fmt.Errorf("positionsWriter: unrouted field %q", atom.Field)

    2. Normalise atom.EmittedAt to UTC + Round(0) so the same wall
       instant in different time.Locations / with monotonic clock
       reads produces the same map key. The pair-up across the lat
       and lng atomics depends on this normalisation.

    3. Acquire mu. Opportunistically evict TTL-expired entries
       (amortised: only sweeps if now - lastEviction >= evictionInterval).
       Look up or create the (vin, ts) entry. If creating and
       len(pending) >= maxPending, return the locked error
         "positionsWriter[positions]: pending buffer full (max=N)"
       so a producer regression cannot OOM the process.

    4. Merge the new value into the entry. If the entry now has BOTH
       hasLat AND hasLng, snapshot the flushable columns into local
       variables (so the upcoming DB Exec runs without holding the
       mutex), then release mu.

       If the entry does NOT have both lat and lng, release mu and
       return nil — the atomic is buffered, ADR-004 #8 explicitly
       allows transient writer buffering as outcome=ok.

    5. Issue the locked upsert (release lock first, then DB Exec):

         INSERT INTO positions (vehicle_id, ts, lat, lng, heading_deg, gps_state)
         SELECT v.id, $2, $3, $4, $5, $6 FROM vehicles v WHERE v.vin = $1
         ON CONFLICT (vehicle_id, ts) DO UPDATE SET
           lat         = EXCLUDED.lat,
           lng         = EXCLUDED.lng,
           heading_deg = COALESCE(EXCLUDED.heading_deg, positions.heading_deg),
           gps_state   = COALESCE(EXCLUDED.gps_state,   positions.gps_state)

       The COALESCE on heading_deg + gps_state is the LOCKED
       semantic: a re-flush that carries NULL for those columns
       (because they have not been observed yet) MUST NOT wipe the
       prior value. Lat and lng do NOT need COALESCE because the
       writer guarantees both are present at every flush.

    6. Tag.RowsAffected() == 0 -> return PII-clean error:
         "positionsWriter[positions]: vehicle not registered"
       The VIN is deliberately omitted from the message.

    7. db.Exec errors are wrapped with fmt.Errorf("...: %w", err)
       so the router's classifyError chain (which inspects ctx
       cancellation, timeout, postgres error codes) traverses the
       wrap correctly.

    8. The flushed entry is RETAINED in the pending buffer, NOT
       deleted. Late-arriving GpsHeading / GpsState for the same
       (vin, ts) — plausible because in the proto schema GpsState=22
       and GpsHeading=23 land AFTER Location=21 — re-flush via the
       upsert idempotently. The TTL bound makes "retain" memory-
       safe in the worst case.

  Compile-time + runtime assertion that *positionsWriter satisfies
  router.Writer. A drift in router.Writer's method set fails the
  build at the assertion line rather than at first integration.

